A||N||12||KRMFiero||How do I lift the car up?||||1||
Z||000000||KRMFiero||02-17-2001||08:14 PM||||I have everything disconnected (so i think). I am ready to lift my Fiero up and undo the cradle bolts.   I tryed lifting it up with my chain fall but i can not find anything to hook it onto. I only have 1 rolling car jack thing (I might be able to borrow one)  What should i do? Any Ideas? How did you guys take your engine out?<P>Kyle<P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE<P>  <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/fiero.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||bubbajoexx||02-17-2001||08:30 PM||||use 4 large jack stands and a large floor <BR>jack and a engine hoist raise the car as high as possible and set it on the stands hock the engine hoist to the engine and let it carry the load of the engine and cradle then remove the cradle bolts the lower the engine and cradle with the engine hoist <p>[This message has been edited by bubbajoexx (edited 02-17-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000002||Phil||02-17-2001||08:53 PM||||I took off the rear facia and hooked a chain to the bumper support,then lift away.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rockcrawl||02-17-2001||09:07 PM||||I have always hooked a chain across the two holes in the firewall cross-brace. Pull the trunk carpet away from th firewall and you'll see what I'm talking about. An engine lifting chain with the bent-rod hooks works well.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||gabriel||02-17-2001||09:09 PM||||Some people have lifted the Fiero body by attaching a hoist to the truck latch reciever & have not reported problems.<P>  I haven't done that though, so I don't write from personal experience.||||reg||1
Z||000005||EdsB52||02-17-2001||09:59 PM||||I've posted this before and people have scawfed at it, but here goes. It will be a lot of work for one engine replacement, but will pay off for multilpe uses. I wish I had a camera to post pics of this device, but it shouldn't be too difficult to explain. Anyway, picture 2 peices of 3/16" steel plate cut into approx 8" circles w/approx 4" holes cut in the middle of them. These circles have 2 tabs each protruding from them approx 4" apart that are parallel to each other. Drill 3 holes in each of these circles so they will fall over the rear strut towers flatly, and bolt them down. Next, get a peice of steel tube that measures 4" x ?", lay it in the middle of the tabs previously mentioned, and drill holes through each end (tabs and tube match drill). Run a pin or a bolt through the holes and weld a tab on top of that tube for the cherry-picker to grab onto. <P>1. lift the car up<BR>2. knock the rear wheels off<BR>3. lay the craddle down onto the legs of the cherry-picker<BR>4. remove the craddle bolts<BR>5. lift the car over the engine/craddle assy<BR>6. install jack stands in grooves at fwd end of sub frame (that look like they were made for this operation)<BR>7. unhook cherry-picker at top<BR>8. roll engine/craddle out the rear corner of car.<P>I've used this tool/operation outside and in conditions that were less than perfectly flat and have never had a near mishap. The time it took me to engineer this tool and the 50 bucks I put out to have it built was well worth it. If I did Fiero's  for a living, I would perfect this design. I release all patent rights to this tool (unless someone already has one)!||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierosound||02-17-2001||10:14 PM||||I went through the same thing.  The trunk latch bent in my case.  I put a bolt through the dog-bone with large washers to the chain hoist and lifted away...||||reg||10
Z||000007||bHooper||02-17-2001||10:46 PM||||i have always used a 4x4 piece of wood, 2 jackstands, a pair of jacks, a pallet and a set of wheels to move refrigerators.<P>here's a step by step<P>1)jack rear end up (chock front)<BR>2) remove the rear wheels<BR>3) slide the 4x4 under car, just in front of the front cradle mounts<BR>4) lower rear of car onto the pallete, and disconect the cradle bolts and strut bolts (at top of tower)<BR>5) disconect <B>everything that attach's the engine to anything</B> i.e.: hoses, tubes, cables, dogbone, <B>grounding straps</B>, etc.<BR>6) raise the car, one side at a time, very carefully so as to make sure nothing is catching.  the car will begin to "teeter" a bit.<BR>7) place jackstands under 4x4 and slide engine out of way.<BR>8) good luck! <P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||1
Z||000008||KRMFiero||02-17-2001||11:01 PM||||I think i will do it bHooper's way, sounds fairly easy, I'll just try to borrow a jack from someone.<P><BR>thank you for all you postings<BR>Kyle||||reg||10
Z||000009||bHooper||02-18-2001||09:58 AM||||oh yeah, don't forget to drain the collant, b4 disconecting all the hoses.  you have to tilt the car around a bit, to avoid having a mess.||||reg||1
Z||000010||TheFiero64||02-18-2001||11:18 AM||||I've lifted from the trunk latch 4 times. The last time it bent up on me. I like Phil's way. Just remove the bumper cover and lift away.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||10
Z||000011||Carrolles||02-19-2001||08:05 AM||||If you get the front wheels off the ground about 6 inches you won't drive the nose into the pavement when you lift the rear to clear the cradle.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||zeusrekning||02-19-2001||08:54 AM||||read on Archies web page to:<BR>1.lift front off the ground about 6"'s support with jackstands.<BR>2.lift rear of car using lift hooks?? on engine.(don't remember how far)Support with jack stands i think.<BR>3.then it tells you how to roll it out.<P>Be sure to check Archies site cause i may of left something out.||||reg||1
